        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "be191d01-c7ec-5548-9ac7-493727852b25",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr.\n- Products: large-capacity electrical switches, 287Kv disconnecting switch\n- Laws: Rural Electrification Act of 1936\n- Organizations: rural electric cooperative\n- Places: West Coast of the United States, rural areas of the United States\n- Concepts: electrification, rural electrification\n- Facilities: Renwick Generating Plant\n\nFacts:\n- Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr. developed crucial early innovations in large-capacity electrical switches, including the 1000lb. 287Kv disconnecting switch.\n- Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr.'s switch innovations were important in the early spread of electrification on the West Coast of the United States during the 1910s and 1920s.\n- Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr.'s switch innovations became essential to New Deal mass-scale rural electrification efforts in the 1930s.\n- The Rural Electrification Act of 1936 was enacted on May 20, 1936.\n- The Rural Electrification Act of 1936 provided federal loans for the installation of electrical distribution systems in isolated rural areas of the United States.\n- The newly formed rural electric cooperative supplied by the Renwick Generating Plant was funded by the Rural Electrification Act of 1936.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0cecff34-fed2-5d6a-b3e3-b7a480c0f574",
        "text": "Page: Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr.. Sentence: His crucial early innovations in large-capacity electrical switches, including the 1000lb.\nPage: Augustus Jesse Bowie Jr.. Sentence: 287Kv disconnecting switch, were important in the early spread of electrification on the West Coast of the United States during the 1910's-1920s, and became essential to the New Deal's mass-scale rural electrification efforts in the 1930s.\nPage: Rural Electrification Act. Sentence: The Rural Electrification Act of 1936, enacted on May 20, 1936, provided federal loans for the installation of electrical distribution systems to serve isolated rural areas of the United States.\nPage: Renwick Generating Plant. Sentence: At that time the plant started to supply power to a newly formed rural electric cooperative that was funded by the Rural Electrification Act of 1936.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
